    e) Now fit a lasso model to the simulated data using $X, X^2, \dots, X^{10}$ as predictors. Use $10$-fold CV to choose the optimal value of $\lambda$. Create plots of the CV error as a function of \lambda. Report the resulting coefficient estimates and discuss the results obtained.
    
```{r}
# 1. Create the matrix of predictors (X, X^2, ..., X^10)
X_matrix <- model.matrix(Y ~ poly(X, 10, raw = TRUE), data = df_sim)[, -1]

# 2. Fit the Lasso model with 10-fold Cross-Validation
# alpha = 1 specifies the Lasso penalty
library(glmnet)
set.seed(445) # Use the same seed for reproducibility
cv_lasso_fit <- cv.glmnet(X_matrix, Y, alpha = 1, nfolds = 10)

# 3. Store the optimal lambda values
lambda_min <- cv_lasso_fit$lambda.min
lambda_1se <- cv_lasso_fit$lambda.1se

cat("Optimal Lambda (lambda.min): ", lambda_min, "\n")
cat("Optimal Lambda (lambda.1se): ", lambda_1se, "\n")

# Plot CV error vs. log(lambda)
plot(cv_lasso_fit)
title("CV Error vs. log(lambda) for Lasso Regression", line = 2.5)


# Coefficients for lambda.min (best fit)
coef_min <- as.matrix(coef(cv_lasso_fit, s = lambda_min))
cat("\nCoefficients at lambda.min:\n")
print(coef_min[coef_min[, 1] != 0, , drop = FALSE]) # Print non-zero coefficients

# Coefficients for lambda.1se (more parsimonious)
coef_1se <- as.matrix(coef(cv_lasso_fit, s = lambda_1se))
cat("\nCoefficients at lambda.1se:\n")
print(coef_1se[coef_1se[, 1] != 0, , drop = FALSE])
```
For these results, it looks like the Lasso's accuracy using the lambda min
criterion successfully identified the exact number and composition of the true
underlying model predictors while also eliminating the 7 noise variables, X^4-
X^10. This was better than BIC and Cp and R^2 adj as it favored the p= 3 model
showing that its continuous penalty adjustment can often find a more accurate 
model than discrete subset selection methods. 
    
