    b. Perform best subset selection on the training set and plot the training set MSE associated with the best model of each size (**Hint** How do we calculate MSE?).
    
```{r}
# Combine training data into a data frame
train_df <- data.frame(y = y_train, X = X_train)

# Perform best subset selection on the training set
# nvmax = p (20) means check models up to size 20
library(leaps)
regfit_best <- regsubsets(y ~ ., data = train_df, nvmax = p)

# Get the summary object
reg_summary <- summary(regfit_best)

# Calculate Training MSE for each model size
training_MSE <- reg_summary$rss / 100

# Create a data frame for plotting
plot_df <- data.frame(
  p = 1:p, # Number of predictors
  MSE = training_MSE
)

# Plot the Training MSE vs. the number of predictors (p)
par(mfrow = c(1, 1))
plot(plot_df$p, plot_df$MSE,
     type = "b", # Use points and lines
     xlab = "Number of Predictors (p)",
     ylab = "Training MSE",
     main = "Training MSE vs. Model Complexity (Best Subset Selection)",
     pch = 19, col = "blue")
```
    
    c. Plot the test set MSE associated with the best model of each size. Hint, to get the predicted values, use matrix multiplication (`%*%`) to multiply the test data by the fitted coefficients of each model: $X\hat{\beta}$. Note there is no `predict` function for `regsubsets` objects.
    
```{r}
#  Training MSE data (from part b)
plot_df_train <- data.frame(
  p = 1:p, 
  MSE = training_MSE
)


# Create the design matrix for the test set (900 rows, 21 columns)
X_test_design <- cbind(Intercept = 1, X_test) 
full_names <- c("(Intercept)", paste0("X.", 1:p)) # Set required names based on error message
colnames(X_test_design) <- full_names 

test_MSE <- rep(NA, p) 

# Loop through all model sizes (i = 1 to 20)
for (i in 1:p) {
  beta_hat_partial <- coef(regfit_best, id = i)
  beta_hat_full <- rep(0, p + 1)
  names(beta_hat_full) <- full_names 
  
  selected_names <- names(beta_hat_partial)
  col_indices <- match(selected_names, full_names) 

 
  beta_hat_full[col_indices] <- beta_hat_partial
  
  y_pred <- X_test_design %*% beta_hat_full
  test_MSE[i] <- mean((y_test - y_pred)^2)
}

plot_df_test <- data.frame(
  p = 1:p,
  MSE = test_MSE
)
# Find the minimum and maximum y-values to set the plot limits
y_min <- min(plot_df_train$MSE, plot_df_test$MSE)
y_max <- max(plot_df_train$MSE, plot_df_test$MSE)

par(mfrow = c(1, 1))
plot(plot_df_test$p, plot_df_test$MSE,
     type = "b",
     xlab = "Number of Predictors (p)",
     ylab = "Mean Squared Error (MSE)",
     main = "Bias-Variance Trade-Off: Training vs. Test Error",
     pch = 19, col = "red",
     ylim = c(y_min * 0.95, y_max * 1.05))

#  the Training MSE line
lines(plot_df_train$p, plot_df_train$MSE, type = "b", pch = 17, col = "blue")

# highlighting the Minimum Test MSE (Optimal Model Size)
min_p <- which.min(plot_df_test$MSE)
points(min_p, min(plot_df_test$MSE), col = "black", pch = 21, bg = "red", cex = 2)

# Add Legend
legend("topright", 
       legend = c("Test MSE (Bias-Variance Trade-off)", "Training MSE (Always Decreases)"), 
       col = c("red", "blue"), pch = c(19, 17), lty = 1)
```
    
    d. For which model size does the test set MSE take its minimum value? Comment on your results.
The minimum test set MSE takes its minimum value at the model size indicated
by the large, black circled point on the line, at about 8 predictors.
These results  clearly demonstrate the core concept of the bias variance
trade off, where at low complexity (p < 8), the test MSE is high bc the model is just
too simple (High bias). This excludes several predictors that are important 
which lead to systematic prediction errors.  At p = 8 the Test MSE reaches
its minimum where it has balanced the trade off. For high complexity, p >8, 
now it is too complex and suffers from overfitting and poor generalization
for the test set. 


    e. How does the model at which the test set MSE is minimized compare to the true model used to generate the data? Comment on the coefficient values.
    
The model that minimizes the Test MSE is the model with p=8 predictors.
This model perfectly matches the true complexity of the data-generating process.
Where the true model has p = 20 predictors but 8 have non zero coeffs,
and the optimal model with the minimum MSE selected 8 predictors. 
While the specific coefficient values for the p = 8 model won't be identical
to the true beta values, they are very close, just slightly different due
to the random noise included in the simulated data and are non zero. 
The coeffs for the 12 excluded predictors will be exactly 0 and are 
exactly 0 in the true model. 
    
